.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
See the module.
.PP
\fBClipWindow\fR: shows its content through a window of a given height and \fBtranslates\fR it instead of squeezing it.
.PP
A plain container with a smaller fixed height gives its child less room, so the child \fBreflows\fR: a cover shrinks, a row wraps, a column squashes. That is the right answer for a panel that is being resized and the wrong one for a panel that is being *revealed*, where the content should keep its size and slide into view. Chakra's \fBCollapse\fR, Mantine's \fBCollapse\fR and \fBGtkRevealer\fR all measure the content at its natural size and move it; this does the same.
.IP \(bu 2
The content is laid out with all the height it wants.
.IP \(bu 2
The window is \fBheight\fR tall, and shows the content from its \fBtop\fR (shrinking the window cuts the bottom off) or from its \fBbottom\fR (shrinking cuts the top off: the content is pushed up).
.IP \(bu 2
Anything outside the window is clipped, drawn or hit.
.PP
The height is the application's (animate it with an \fBAnimation\fR or a \fBReveal\fR); the window just follows.
.PP
.nf
.RS
ClipWindow::new(queue_list).height(self.reveal * list_height).anchor(Anchor::Bottom)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Without it, the same effect is a non\-scrolling \fBscrollable\fR with a hidden scrollbar and \fBanchor_bottom\fR, which is how this was first written.
